{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001 restricts its collapse argument to the case where 'the checklist is strictly a surface-correlational heuristic,' then generalizes that 'no single evaluation can separate them.' But the problem asks what evidence distinguishes genuine reliability from compliance *in the general case*, not merely under a stipulation that forecloses the distinction by definition. If the checklist is not a mere surface heuristic but encodes verifiable causal steps, the 'collapses' claim no longer follows, yet SRC_001 never establishes why every checklist must be surface-correlational; its countercondition only covers the panel treating a surface checklist as a reasoning trigger, not the checklist itself having causal content. The argument therefore sidesteps the core question rather than answering it.","target":"62c980263e151a972e856e99388e2b2f5fc6b4a3870fd2912ca7f2f4a4d37ff1"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002 claims process-tracing 'merely relocates the confound' because integrated reasoning is consistent with a more sophisticated correlation strategy. But the refutation condition it states—uniquely decomposing integrated reasoning into causal-structure inference versus correlation exploitation via verbal protocol or response-time signature—is actually plausible within a single evaluation: causal-model questions can be designed so that correct answers are uncorrelated with the surface cues the correlation strategy would exploit, making the decomposition empirically achievable. By framing this as merely a refutation condition rather than recognizing it may be satisfiable, SRC_002's compatibility claim is self-undermining—if the refutation obtains, process-tracing does resolve the confound, contradicting the claim that it 'relocates' it.","target":"71f02b5000e98b9dd10ef996360f41b9701691704f40f62a586fc7a954f8fbec"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_003's circularity charge conflates two different epistemic roles. Constructing an adversarial item where surface cues and causal structure diverge requires knowing the causal structure of *that item*, but the reliability claim being evaluated concerns *average performance across a domain*. Knowing the ground-truth label for specific test items is exactly what 'objectively verifiable outcomes' provides—it is not circular to use known answers to probe whether a panel's process tracks structure rather than surface cues. The 'presupposes knowledge of that structure' objection treats item-level ground truth (available by problem stipulation) as if it were the same latent causal model whose detection is at issue, collapsing a legitimate verification step into an alleged circularity.","target":"c0ca57a8720e69b75bb30f53b2111dbc0df413b377b30c18aab086f6fdda9dd2"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_004 frames the panel-vs-expert comparison as a product of (theory quality, execution fidelity) and claims aggregation is not a separate factor. But this is refuted by a basic Condorcet mechanism: if N non-expert executors each have error rate p<1/2 *independently*, majority-vote reliability approaches 1 as N grows, even holding theory quality and individual execution fidelity fixed. This aggregation boost is neither a theory-quality improvement nor a per-executor fidelity improvement; it is a structural property of the voting layer under independence. SRC_004's own refutation condition names exactly this ('a reliability boost not factorizable into either theory quality or execution fidelity'), yet the Condorcet result shows that condition is satisfied, so the claimed 'dissolves into product' framing fails for the panel case specifically.","target":"c5a21daa3ba7b7b340bbb06713128184c0830cf6b94a6b251672c2651ce4bda2"}]}